"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[6429],{16429:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"slug":"nobel-ekonomika-2026-holl-vudford-ozhidaniya","locale":"en","title":"Economics Nobel awarded for models helping central banks account for household expectations","description":"Hall and Woodford received the 2026 economics Nobel. Their models help explain why the Bank of Russia\'s rate stays at 14% while inflation is 7.17%.","blocks":[{"id":"b001","kind":"paragraph","text":"On October 12, 2026, the Nobel committee awarded the economics prize to Robert Hall of Stanford University and Michael Woodford of Columbia University for the microeconomic foundations of macroeconomic stabilization. They will share 12 million Swedish kronor, about $1.2 million. The committee says models developed from their work are now used by central banks and finance ministries worldwide."},{"id":"b002","kind":"paragraph","text":"Stabilization is what central banks and governments do: slow an overheating economy when prices rise and stimulate it when unemployment increases. Almost 100 years ago, John Maynard Keynes proposed measuring this through relationships between aggregate figures such as inflation, unemployment and consumption. Those relationships broke down in the 1970s. The US tried to reduce unemployment by stimulating demand but ended up with unemployment and high inflation together."},{"id":"b003","kind":"paragraph","text":"Hall and Woodford built models from the bottom up, starting with decisions by individuals and companies. In a 1978 paper developing Milton Friedman\'s ideas, Hall showed that spending depends on expected future income, not just today\'s wages. The central conclusion is that the economy responds both to what happens and to what people expect. By explaining its plans, a central bank can alter those expectations."},{"id":"b004","kind":"paragraph","text":"A landlord illustrates how expectations move prices. If they expect everything to become 14% more expensive over a year, they raise the rent immediately rather than a year later. A tenant expecting price increases raises prices for their own services. Prices consequently rise because people expect them to rise. These are inflation expectations."},{"id":"b005","kind":"paragraph","text":"The gap is currently large in Russia. The Economy Ministry estimated annual inflation at 7.17% on October 5, whereas the Bank of Russia\'s September survey showed households expecting 14.2% over the following year. The Bank of Russia publishes this survey monthly and follows it in rate decisions. While people expect prices to rise almost 2 times faster than they actually are, the regulator fears temporary jumps, such as those caused by fuel, will become persistent."},{"id":"b006","kind":"paragraph","text":"For an ordinary person, this Nobel prize implies something straightforward: the Bank of Russia\'s rate depends not only on current inflation but on what people believe. While expectations remain high, loans stay expensive and deposits attractive. Waiting to buy on credit costs time; borrowing now costs high interest. The Bank of Russia will announce its rate decision on October 23 and will examine household expectations as closely as prices themselves."},{"id":"b007","kind":"heading","text":"Who received the economics Nobel in 2026?"},{"id":"b008","kind":"paragraph","text":"American economists Robert Hall of Stanford and Michael Woodford of Columbia University. The prize was awarded on October 12, 2026, for the microeconomic foundations of macroeconomic stabilization; they will share 12 million Swedish kronor."},{"id":"b009","kind":"heading","text":"What was the 2026 economics Nobel awarded for?"},{"id":"b010","kind":"paragraph","text":"Models in which the economy emerges from decisions by individuals and companies, and those decisions depend on expectations about the future. Central banks and finance ministries use such models to decide when to slow or stimulate the economy."},{"id":"b011","kind":"heading","text":"How is the Nobel prize connected to the Bank of Russia\'s rate?"},{"id":"b012","kind":"paragraph","text":"The laureates\' models explain how expectations move prices. Annual Russian inflation is 7.17%, while households expect 14.2%. With expectations high, a temporary price spike can become persistent, so the Bank of Russia is in no hurry to cut its 14% rate."}],"sources":[{"label":"Nobel prize in economics awarded to Robert E. Hall and Michael Woodford","url":"https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/12/nobel-economics-prize-winner-woodford-hall.html"},{"label":"Who received the economics Nobel and why","url":"https://www.vedomosti.ru/economics/articles/2026/10/13/1236487-kto-poluchil-nobelevskuyu-premiyu-po-ekonomike"},{"label":"The triumph of New Keynesian economics: why this year\'s economics Nobel was awarded","url":"https://thebell.io/triumf-novokeynsianstva-za-chto-dali-nobelya-po-ekonomike-v-etom-godu"},{"label":"Inflation expectations and consumer sentiment, September 2026","url":"https://www.cbr.ru/analytics/dkp/inflationary_expectations/Infl_exp_26-09/"},{"label":"Russian inflation from September 29 to October 5","url":"https://www.vedomosti.ru/economics/news/2026/10/07/1235202-inflyatsiya-v-rossii"}],"sourceBodySha256":"e13ad2b2a2ae1a90ea84da9b64778750943d70fe565916eb1fb9a1a889724ba6"}')}}]);